--- ANSWER ---
**Answer:** `Boolean.Mechanical Action` is property ID **0x6333808**. It is a **U32**, **read/write**, and takes values **0–5**. `Traverse for GObjects.vi` **does return front-panel objects** when its `Traverse Target` is set to FP; this project's code always sets it to BD.

### 1. Mechanical Action
- **(a) ID:** `6333808` hex. Both labviewwiki pages list it: the [Boolean class page](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Boolean_class) and the [Mechanical_Action_property page](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Boolean_class/Mechanical_Action_property). Its short name is `MechAction`.
- **(b) Type and permission:** labviewwiki says U32, Read/Write, "RTE: No". Its property page adds that the value **cannot be changed while the VI is running**, but remote access is allowed. A search snippet of NI's page ([ni.com mechanicalaction.html](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-US/bundle/labview-api-ref/page/properties-and-methods/vi-server/generic/gobject/control/boolean/mechanicalaction.html)) agrees: values 0–5, read/write, not available in the Run-Time Engine, cannot be set while the VI runs. I could not tell from the sources whether the U32 is presented as an enum or a ring.
- **(c) Value mapping:** I could not read NI's own number-to-name table. ni.com renders that page with JavaScript, and WebFetch only got the navigation menu, three times. labviewwiki gives the rule in words: values 0–5 are the six options "arranged from top left to bottom right" in the Boolean's shortcut-menu palette. Reading that rule against the usual palette layout (Switch actions on the top row, Latch actions on the bottom) gives:
  - 0 = Switch When Pressed
  - 1 = Switch When Released
  - 2 = Switch Until Released
  - 3 = Latch When Pressed
  - 4 = Latch When Released
  - 5 = Latch Until Released

  **Treat this list as a strong inference, not a quoted table.** Two cheap ways to settle it: read the property on a Boolean whose action you already know, or open the NI page in a real browser. labviewwiki's [Boolean_controls](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Boolean_controls) page lists the actions in the same order but gives no numbers.

### 2. Class ID and neighbouring properties
The Boolean class ID on labviewwiki is **"8"**. The neighbouring properties all sit in one ID block, `6333801`–`633380C`:

| ID (hex) | Property | Access |
|---|---|---|
| 6333801 | Button Size | read/write |
| 6333802 | Button Size:Width | read/write |
| 6333803 | Button Size:Height | read/write |
| 6333804 | Strings [4] | read/write |
| 6333805 | Colors [4] | read/write |
| 6333806 | Boolean Text | read only |
| 6333807 | Lock Boolean Text In Center | read/write |
| **6333808** | **Mechanical Action** | **read/write (RTE: No)** |
| 6333809 | IsTracking | read only |
| 633380A | Multiple Text String | read/write |
| 633380B | Toggle Key Binding | read/write |
| 633380C | Decal Scaling? | read/write |

The Boolean class has no methods of its own; it inherits them from Control.

### 3. Traverse for GObjects
It returns **front-panel objects** when you ask for the front panel:
- [LabVIEW Artisan, Scripting Tip #1](http://labviewartisan.blogspot.com/2009/06/labview-scripting-tip-1-power-of.html) names three targets: "BD" (block diagram), "FP" (front panel), and "Other" with an `Other Refnum`. The search is recursive, so it finds objects nested inside containers.
- The connector list for this VI is recorded at `docs/NAMES.md:881`. It includes `Traverse Target`, `Other Refnum`, `Class Name` and `VI Refnum`.
- Our own code sets the target to BD at `tools/gscript.py:1880`, with the comment `1 = BD (0 = FP)`. So setting `Traverse Target` = 0 with `Class Name` = "Boolean" should return the panel Booleans. **Nothing in this project has run that yet.** `docs/main-vi-stop-and-save.md:94` lists a recursive front-panel census as a test that has not been built.
- **Name correction:** the LabVIEW 2026 `traverseref.llb` has no VI called `TRef Traverse for GObjects.vi`. It contains `Traverse for GObjects.vi`, `TRef Traverse.vi`, `TRef Traverse for References.vi` and `VI Scripting - Traverse.lvlib`. The Artisan post names `TRef Traverse for References.vi` as the generic version.

### Things to watch for the planned reader (the one in `task_138-5.json:5`)
- The bed VI must not be running when the property is read. Write access is blocked during a run, and it is safer to assume reads may be too.
- To read Mechanical Action you need a reference **typed as `Boolean`**. The usual pattern is `Class Name`="Boolean" with the FP target, or a cast to the more specific class. `docs/NAMES.md:395` and `docs/d1-build-plan.md:752` record that refs from `Traverse for GObjects` come out typed `GObject` and could not feed some typed nodes. Plan for that cast instead of assuming the reference will wire straight in.

